    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d2">

                <pb facs="0013" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg1"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 Steering Committee/507: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-04-14T17:00:00-05:00">April 14, 1937—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>25. Your letter of April 6 to Dunn.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d2-f1"/> We would like
                    to have your views on Norman Davis’<anchor n="3" xml:id="d2-f2"/> proceeding to
                    Geneva for meeting of Disarmament Bureau May 6. Decision has been left to Mr.
                    Davis. While this Government has no desire to take any initiative at Bureau
                    meeting, we do not desire that any impression should be given of any lack of
                    interest on our part in the question.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d2-f1" n="2" xml:id="d2-f1-content">
                <p>James C. Dunn, Chief of the Division of Western European Affairs; letter not
                    found in Department files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d2-f2" n="3" xml:id="d2-f2-content">
                <p>Chairman of the American delegation, Conference for the Reduction and Limitation
                    of Armaments, Geneva; Mr. Davis was in London as Chairman of the American
                    delegation to the International Sugar Conference (see <ref target="#pg931">pp.
                        931</ref> ff.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
